.el .ds Aq '
.TH ifg 1  "ifg 0.8.0" 
.SH NAME
ifg \- Work with IronForge repositories, issues, pull requests, CI and releases from the terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBifg\fR [\fB\-\-host\fR] [\fB\-R\fR|\fB\-\-repo\fR] [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-q\fR|\fB\-\-template\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-color\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR]... [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] [\fB\-V\fR|\fB\-\-version\fR] <\fIsubcommands\fR>
.SH DESCRIPTION
Work with IronForge repositories, issues, pull requests, CI and releases from the terminal.
.PP
`ifg` talks to the IronForge REST API with a personal access token. The token is validated by `auth login`, stored in `$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ironforge/hosts.toml` (or the system keychain when `token_store = "keyring"`), and reused by every command.
.PP
Machine\-readable output: `\-\-json` prints the server\*(Aqs own field names, `\-q/\-\-template` renders a small template per result (`{{.number}} {{.title}}`, `{{range .labels}}{{.}} {{end}}`). Colors, relative timestamps and the pager only switch on when stdout is a terminal.
.PP
Long\-tail API surface (wiki, webhooks, packages, secrets, …) is reachable through `ifg api <path>`.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-host\fR \fI<NAME|URL>\fR
Host name from the config, or a base URL like https://forge.example.com

Resolution order: this flag, `IRONFORGE_URL`, the repository\*(Aqs git remote (when run inside a checkout), then `default_host` from the config file.
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-repo\fR \fI<OWNER/NAME>\fR
Target repository as OWNER/NAME, or a full remote URL

Inside a git checkout `ifg` infers OWNER/NAME from `origin`, so the flag is only needed outside a repository or to target another one.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output JSON (server field names) instead of a table

Field names are the server\*(Aqs own, so `jq` expressions from the API docs work unchanged. Mutually exclusive with `\-\-template`.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-template\fR \fI<TEMPLATE>\fR
Render each result through a small template (`{{.number}} {{.title}}`, nested paths allowed; lists render once per row)

Supported subset: `{{.field}}`, `{{.nested.field}}`, `{{.array.0}}`, `{{range .list}}…{{end}}` (nestable; objects iterate their values) and the escapes `\\n` / `\\t`. Missing fields render empty. Lists render the template once per item, so `{{range}}` is only needed for nested arrays such as an issue\*(Aqs labels.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-color\fR
Disable colored output (also honors NO_COLOR)
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Verbosity: \-v logs each request, \-vv also logs headers
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
ifg\-auth(1)
Manage credentials for IronForge hosts
.TP
ifg\-api(1)
Raw pass\-through to the IronForge REST API (paths relative to /api/v1/)
.TP
ifg\-repo(1)
Work with repositories
.TP
ifg\-issue(1)
Work with issues
.TP
ifg\-pr(1)
Work with pull requests (`checkout` fetches the head branch)
.TP
ifg\-ci(1)
Work with CI pipelines
.TP
ifg\-release(1)
Work with releases and their assets
.TP
ifg\-label(1)
Work with repository labels
.TP
ifg\-milestone(1)
Work with repository milestones
.TP
ifg\-status(1)
Show the current branch\*(Aqs pull request and recent pipeline runs
.TP
ifg\-search(1)
Search repositories, issues and wiki pages
.TP
ifg\-org(1)
Work with organizations
.TP
ifg\-notifications(1)
List and manage your notifications
.TP
ifg\-version(1)
Print the client version and (when reachable) the server version
.TP
ifg\-completions(1)
Print a shell completion script (bash / zsh / fish / powershell)
.TP
ifg\-help(1)
Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)
.SH EXTRA
EXAMPLES:
  ifg auth login \-\-host https://forge.example.com   store a token for a host
  ifg issue list \-R alice/demo \-\-state open        list open issues
  ifg pr checkout 12                               fetch a PR into pr\-12
  ifg ci logs 7 \-\-follow                           stream a job log
  ifg pr list \-q \*(Aq{{.number}} {{.title}}\*(Aq           script\-friendly output
  ifg api users/me                                 raw API passthrough

Run `ifg help <command>` for the full description of one command,
or `man ifg` / `man ifg\-<command>` when the man pages are installed.
